-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Eviction
+
+The cache is partitioned into a probationary queue and a protected queue. Their configured byte capacities sum to 12 GiB.
+
+- **Probationary queue**: receives every sheet on first admission. Entries leave in first-in-first-out order. Default capacity: 3 GiB (25% of the cache, about 1,500 sheets).
+- **Protected queue**: holds sheets that have been requested at least twice while cached. Entries leave in least-recently-used order. Default capacity: 9 GiB (75% of the cache, about 4,600 sheets).
+
+A cache hit in the probationary queue promotes the sheet to the protected queue and marks it most recently used. A cache hit in the protected queue marks it most recently used without moving it. A completed render is admitted to the back of the probationary queue; while that queue exceeds its capacity, sheets are evicted from its front. A promotion that would exceed the protected queue's capacity first evicts the least recently used protected sheet.
+
+Long screenings cycle one-use sheets through the probationary queue. Comparison sheets that editors have already requested twice sit in the protected queue and are not displaced by those one-use sheets. A screening sheet that happens to be requested twice is promoted, so the policy protects demonstrated reuse rather than trying to recognize a particular production's comparison set.
+
+The capacities are startup configuration, not a per-request input. No other node reads queues, counters, or eviction metadata.
+
+### Eviction Metadata
+
+Each cached entry carries its queue membership and recency position. This metadata is bounded by the cached entry count and discarded with the entry. It does not survive a worker restart; every restart begins with empty queues.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q: FIFO probationary queue plus LRU protected queue | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q keeps one-use screening sheets from evicting comparison sheets that editors request repeatedly. LRU lets a long screening flush the comparison set; aged frequency counts add counter maintenance and can keep yesterday's favorites too long. |
+| Queue size split | 3 GiB probationary / 9 GiB protected, tunable at startup | Smaller probationary queue; even split | A 25/75 split leaves most of the cache for the reused comparison set while giving each screening enough probationary room to absorb its scan. The exact split is deployment tuning, not a code change. |
+| Protected-queue order | Least recently used | First-in-first-out | Editors return to recent comparisons; LRU keeps that recent set and evicts the oldest protected sheet. |
+| Promotion trigger | Second request for a cached key | First admission; explicit pin or unpin | A second touch separates screening scans from comparison working sets. Explicit pinning would expose replacement policy to other nodes and break the worker-private bookkeeping boundary. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Eviction policy: 2Q. It remains internal to the worker; the external interface, render concurrency, and disk budget do not change.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Exact queue split under production traces. Start at 25/75 and tune from cache-hit and eviction data.
+2. Protection for a comparison sheet's first screening pass. 2Q promotes only on a second cached request; a sheet evicted before that second request receives no protection. Revisit only if production traces show comparison sheets lost this way.
